title: Valuable Insights
---
body:

### Less is More

"Less is more" is a popular expression, and it resonates with me. I often hear this phrase from my English tutor, Mark. I remember we did an interesting exercise in one lesson — reading a text where I had to remove unnecessary words from sentences while keeping the meaning unchanged. Such a simple life principle — throw away everything unnecessary, but keep what's most valuable!

I think it would be good to make this my main principle in life. So I'll try to structure this from general to specific, from everyday routine to professional. What exactly I see in this deep philosophical idea:

- **Goals.** Focus on what matters most. When there are too many ideas and tasks, it's important to separate what's truly significant right now, and move step by step.
- **Household.** It would be wrong to think that the space we live in doesn't affect us. Everyone knows that the process of cleaning and throwing away unnecessary things brings great satisfaction.
- **Surroundings.** Same as with the household :) Life is too short to spend it on people who don't bring us joy or meaning. We should appreciate time and spend it only with "our people."
- **Work.** In software development, there is even a special term — *Pythonic* code. It means writing code that is not just correct, but elegant and readable. The idea is simple: a good programmer doesn't try to show off with complex constructions, but instead writes code that is easy to read and understand. Less noise, more clarity.
- To push our changes to a remote server via Git, we need to provide a clear description of the changes — what we did and why. A lot depends on this, and it's often quite a challenge to do this concisely and informatively. The same goes for code — it should be clean and optimized. I'm still learning this from my senior colleagues, but I often notice that writing less code is my mission :)
- **Art.** I often like minimalism in interior design and clothing. I love minimalist postcards and even flower bouquets. It's in this style that I truly enjoy the details. As for photography — I love black and white photos. They contain a lot of meaning and authenticity without excessive noise.
